00:17:48,460 --> 00:17:54,620
The magnet separates the wire, directs it onto a conveyor belt where it goes outside to a recycling bin.

228
00:17:55,680 --> 00:18:02,520
The resulting clean rubber, called crumb, is used in sports fields, rubber hoses and mats, and asphalt.

229
00:18:02,521 --> 00:18:14,140
At minus 320 degrees Fahrenheit, liquid nitrogen is so cold that it turns the water vapor from the surrounding air into small water droplets, creating giant clouds of

230
00:18:14,141 --> 00:18:14,841
fog.

231
00:18:15,480 --> 00:18:18,860
And this mysterious smoky fluid can even liquefy your breath.

232
00:18:19,660 --> 00:18:25,940
Take a look at my breath inside the balloon, actually as a liquid. Look at this.

233
00:18:25,941 --> 00:18:33,400
Extreme cold can even make some materials like tin and aluminum take on a sort of superpower.

234
00:18:36,040 --> 00:18:42,120
When chilled down far enough, they become superconductors and transmit electricity without any resistance.

235
00:18:42,700 --> 00:18:49,760
At that point, the electrons that flow through the metal have this special property where they can pair up with one another.

236
00:18:49,900 --> 00:18:51,940
It's almost like they're dancing with each other.

237
00:18:51,941 --> 00:18:55,800
and as they're flying through the metal and they come upon different ions,

238
00:18:56,180 --> 00:19:00,820
they can help each other move around and simply flow through the metal without hitting anything.

239
00:19:01,920 --> 00:19:07,360
Once set in motion, electrical current will flow forever in a closed loop of superconducting material.

240
00:19:09,280 --> 00:19:15,060
A visionary application for superconductors is to employ them in the transmission of commercial power to cities.

241
00:19:15,061 --> 00:19:25,140
Before the mid-1980s, superconductors only worked using liquid helium at minus 452 degrees Fahrenheit.

242
00:19:26,460 --> 00:19:29,840
And maintaining such a low temperature was not cost effective.

243
00:19:31,020 --> 00:19:38,440
But the creation of ceramic-based materials has allowed for the use of liquid nitrogen in high-temperature superconducting cables,

244
00:19:39,100 --> 00:19:42,080
if you consider minus 320 degrees Fahrenheit high.

245
00:19:42,081 --> 00:19:46,980
One of the major challenges with our grid is getting power to where it's needed most.

246
00:19:47,760 --> 00:19:53,560
HTS cables, because of their power density, can deliver three to five times the amount of electricity a conventional cable.

247
00:19:54,820 --> 00:20:03,040
In July 2006, the world's first in-grid high-temperature superconducting power cable was installed in Albany, New York.

248
00:20:03,940 --> 00:20:10,720
The 34.5-kilovolt cable runs a quarter of a mile, connecting two substations in the New York grid.

249
00:20:10,721 --> 00:20:15,040
It transmits enough power for 32,000 homes.

250
00:20:15,041 --> 00:20:20,180
This is the HTS cable as it is leaving the ground at the end of its quarter-mile run,

251
00:20:20,280 --> 00:20:22,180
just before it is connected to the termination.

252
00:20:22,640 --> 00:20:23,440
Here is a cutaway.

253
00:20:23,940 --> 00:20:26,720
This inside cavity here is filled with liquid nitrogen.

254
00:20:27,240 --> 00:20:31,080
The copper-colored material here is the actual HTS material.

255
00:20:31,900 --> 00:20:34,120
All the electricity flows through this material.

256
00:20:34,700 --> 00:20:38,420
At the termination, the HTS cable meets the conventional cables.

257
00:20:38,421 --> 00:20:45,420
The electricity flows onto customers, while the liquid nitrogen flows back to a refrigeration system to be recooled.

258
00:20:45,421 --> 00:20:49,420
That happens here in this cold box.

259
00:20:49,421 --> 00:20:55,420
Inside this cold box is a thermosiphon, which contains a bath of liquid nitrogen.

260
00:20:55,421 --> 00:20:59,420
That acts as a heat exchanger, cooling down the warmed-up nitrogen.

261
00:20:59,421 --> 00:21:07,420
If we're able to find energy sources, you know, huge banks of solar panels that are far away,

262
00:21:07,421 --> 00:21:11,420
we should be able to shift that energy through superconducting power cables

263
00:21:11,421 --> 00:21:15,420
without losing much of it. The efficiency goes way up.

264
00:21:15,421 --> 00:21:21,420
In addition to HTS cables, superconductivity can be used to create magnetism.

265
00:21:21,421 --> 00:21:25,420
This little disc is actually a superconductor, and a superconductor is a special material

266
00:21:25,421 --> 00:21:29,420
that will conduct electricity at very, very low temperatures.

267
00:21:29,421 --> 00:21:35,420
But the superconductor actually creates this magnetic field around it.

268
00:21:35,421 --> 00:21:45,420
Since the superconductor has no electrical resistance, this induced current continues to flow, keeping the magnet suspended indefinitely.

269
00:21:45,421 --> 00:21:50,420
This cold boost may be used in future transport vehicles.

270
00:21:50,421 --> 00:21:56,420
In fact, superconducting maglev trains are already being tested on tracks in Japan.

271
00:21:56,421 --> 00:22:03,420
In the mid-1980s, pieces of the head of the Statue of Liberty were cryogenically processed with liquid nitrogen.

272
00:22:03,421 --> 00:22:09,420
This deep freeze treatment strengthened and extended the life of the century-old metal.

273
00:22:09,421 --> 00:22:13,420
Deep Freeze will return on Modern Marvels.

274
00:22:14,420 --> 00:22:20,420
It's tough enough to clean the dirtiest grime, yet gentle enough to restore irreplaceable artifacts.

275
00:22:22,420 --> 00:22:25,420
Blasted onto any surface, it disappears on contact.

276
00:22:25,421 --> 00:22:30,420
And at minus 109 degrees Fahrenheit, it's extremely cold.

277
00:22:30,421 --> 00:22:34,558
cold. The magic ingredient that's making it all happen?

278
00:22:34,559 --> 00:22:36,740
Frozen carbon dioxide, better

279
00:22:36,741 --> 00:22:38,180
known as dry ice.

280
00:22:38,181 --> 00:22:43,300
We put it into a system that's sort of similar to a sandblasting unit, and we propel the

281
00:22:43,301 --> 00:22:48,640
dry ice from rates of 600 feet per second to about 1,000 feet per second. And as it

282
00:22:48,641 --> 00:22:51,758
strikes a solid item, it sublimates directly into a gas.

283
00:22:51,759 --> 00:22:53,540
There is no evaporation process,

284
00:22:53,541 --> 00:22:57,276
it's directly solid to gas. Demonstrated like this.

285
00:22:57,277 --> 00:22:59,840
Small quantity of dry ice at a high

286
00:22:59,841 --> 00:23:05,480
velocity into a solid item and it just goes into a gas. The frigid temperature

287
00:23:05,481 --> 00:23:09,680
of the dry ice blasting against the grime causes it to shrink and lose

288
00:23:09,681 --> 00:23:16,160
adhesion from its subsurface. As that happens the dry ice explodes into vapor

289
00:23:16,161 --> 00:23:21,340
expanding 800 times its original volume. So there's a mass of the dry ice

290
00:23:21,341 --> 00:23:26,280
striking the surface, the extreme cold and this explosion that is helping to

291
00:23:26,281 --> 00:23:31,860
remove the contaminants. The process has been used to renovate nearly 100-year-old

292
00:23:31,861 --> 00:23:36,120
roof tiles at the Philadelphia Museum of Art.

293
00:23:36,121 --> 00:23:40,280
Dry ice blast cleaning removed the built-up tar without scratching the

294
00:23:40,281 --> 00:23:46,500
irreplaceable tiles, each worth an estimated $10,000. It's even used to

295
00:23:46,501 --> 00:23:51,780
strip crystal buildup in wine barrels and to restore smoke-damaged buildings.

296
00:23:51,781 --> 00:23:56,780
Don't want to get your antique book wet while cleaning it? No problem.

297
00:23:56,781 --> 00:24:03,780
You can see as we're cleaning this book, small particles of dry ice are coming out,

298
00:24:03,781 --> 00:24:07,518
more or less just dusting the surface of the book.

299
00:24:07,519 --> 00:24:11,780
Doesn't damage any surfaces that are harder than dry ice.

300
00:24:11,781 --> 00:24:17,780
Dry ice on a scale of 1 to 10 is about a 2, with diamond being a 10.

301
00:24:17,781 --> 00:24:21,360
Of course, when people think of dry ice, they don't think of cleaning.

302
00:24:23,260 --> 00:24:26,580
Dry ice is more commonly known for its ability to keep things cold.

303
00:24:27,280 --> 00:24:34,220
When you're packaging frozen items, it is the only portable form of refrigeration that will actually keep a frozen item frozen.

304
00:24:34,720 --> 00:24:38,400
It has twice the refrigeration per unit weight as water ice.

305
00:24:38,760 --> 00:24:43,160
When you're packaging with water ice, it melts at 32 degrees Fahrenheit,

306
00:24:43,460 --> 00:24:46,100
so anything that freezes at a temperature lower than that will melt.

307
00:24:46,101 --> 00:24:52,100
Air Gas Incorporated is the leading producer of dry ice in the United States.

308
00:24:52,101 --> 00:24:57,100
At their dry ice plant in Hopewell, Virginia, they start with recycled carbon dioxide.

309
00:24:57,101 --> 00:25:04,100
The CO2 gas that we process here is a byproduct from an ammonia fertilizer production process.

310
00:25:04,101 --> 00:25:09,100
Essentially that gas that's created is brought into this facility, cleaned up, and reused.

311
00:25:09,101 --> 00:25:15,820
Air gas liquefies the carbon dioxide vapor using a series of compression and cooling cycles.

312
00:25:16,460 --> 00:25:20,600
Then, the liquid carbon dioxide enters the dry ice plant.

313
00:25:21,160 --> 00:25:22,420
This is our block process.

314
00:25:23,200 --> 00:25:27,400
When the liquid comes in, at this point, it's 250 pounds of querying,

315
00:25:27,520 --> 00:25:29,940
and it's about 8 degrees below zero Fahrenheit.

316
00:25:30,740 --> 00:25:34,180
When the liquid carbon dioxide is injected into the block press,

317
00:25:34,640 --> 00:25:37,320
the pressure is lowered to match the atmosphere,

318
00:25:37,321 --> 00:25:40,380
and the temperature drops 101 degrees.

319
00:26:07,320 --> 00:26:12,320
Once it comes out in the back of the press, it goes down the line.

320
00:26:12,321 --> 00:26:16,320
The two lime saws that cut that to four 50-pound blocks.

321
00:26:16,321 --> 00:26:24,320
We have the capacity to make 3,600 of those 50-pound blocks in a 24-hour period.

322
00:26:24,321 --> 00:26:29,320
Temperatures on Earth have dipped as low as minus 129 degrees Fahrenheit.

323
00:26:29,321 --> 00:26:32,320
So why doesn't it snow dry ice?

324
00:26:32,321 --> 00:26:38,680
While carbon dioxide in the air will freeze below minus 109 degrees Fahrenheit, the concentration

325
00:26:38,681 --> 00:26:44,000
of the dry ice particles is so low that they are not visible.

326
00:26:44,001 --> 00:26:45,758
Dry ice plants across the U.S.

327
00:26:45,759 --> 00:26:49,040
run around the clock, forming blocks, as well as pellets

328
00:26:49,041 --> 00:26:55,940
like those used in blast cleaning to meet the demands of numerous industries.

329
00:26:55,941 --> 00:26:59,300
60% of dry ice produced is used in food processing.

330
00:27:00,240 --> 00:27:03,660
Almost 13% by hospitals and pharmaceutical companies.

331
00:27:05,040 --> 00:27:08,220
And blast cleaning makes up nearly 10% of dry ice usage.

332
00:27:09,840 --> 00:27:14,140
Today you can even buy dry ice at over 4,500 U.S. grocery stores.

333
00:27:14,940 --> 00:27:20,440
You're going to need to have some gloves because dry ice is 78 degrees below zero Celsius,

334
00:27:20,441 --> 00:27:22,720
about 110 degrees below zero Fahrenheit.

335
00:27:23,220 --> 00:27:25,180
So it's really, really important that you don't touch it.

336
00:27:25,940 --> 00:27:31,820
If touched briefly, dry ice is harmless, but contact with the skin for more than a second

337
00:27:31,821 --> 00:27:34,180
will freeze cells and cause frostbite.

338
00:27:34,181 --> 00:27:37,180
But you know what everybody likes to do with dry ice?

339
00:27:37,181 --> 00:27:40,420
They like to put it in water because it gives us this great cloud.

340
00:27:40,421 --> 00:27:41,460
So it's pretty simple to do.

341
00:27:41,461 --> 00:27:44,840
Just take your dry ice and of course you just need a little bit of water.

342
00:27:44,841 --> 00:27:48,060
The warmer the water the better because it gives you a better cloud.

343
00:27:48,061 --> 00:27:54,380
And then you just drop the dry ice inside and you get that spooky looking drink.

344
00:27:54,381 --> 00:28:00,260
Now you can tell that it's actually cold because you see how the vapor is coming down, that

345
00:28:00,261 --> 00:28:05,420
carbon dioxide is more dense than the air around it.

346
00:28:05,421 --> 00:28:10,340
Letting the dry ice sublimate into an open space is the only way to dispose of it.

347
00:28:10,341 --> 00:28:16,140
Trying to contain the expanding gas would result in an explosion.

348
00:28:16,141 --> 00:28:21,880
Though carbon dioxide makes up 80% of greenhouse gas emissions in the United States, far less

349
00:28:21,881 --> 00:28:24,240
than 1% of it is from dry ice.

350
00:28:24,241 --> 00:28:28,880
But when you're planning the next Halloween party, remember

351
00:28:29,400 --> 00:28:34,200
that that witch's brew has a carbon footprint, even if it's a small one.

352
00:28:34,201 --> 00:28:39,440
In 1925, Pressed Air Devices was the first company to produce commercial dry ice in the

353
00:28:39,441 --> 00:28:40,529
United States.

354
00:28:40,530 --> 00:28:46,440
Today, the United States produces nearly 2 billion pounds of dry ice a year.

355
00:28:46,441 --> 00:28:50,380
Deep Freeze will return on Modern Marvels.

356
00:28:50,381 --> 00:28:52,920
Minus 10, 9, 8.

357
00:28:53,140 --> 00:28:55,260
August 8, 2007.

358
00:28:57,000 --> 00:29:00,880
Like clockwork, a 120-ton space shuttle Endeavor

359
00:29:00,881 --> 00:29:03,020
lifts majestically off its launch pad.

360
00:29:04,420 --> 00:29:06,560
Giant flames engulf the sky,

361
00:29:07,140 --> 00:29:08,900
and plumes of smoke fill the air.

362
00:29:09,500 --> 00:29:12,480
Heads down, brings level for the 8.5-minute ride to orbit.

363
00:29:12,900 --> 00:29:15,200
It's one of the largest controlled fires on Earth,

364
00:29:15,201 --> 00:29:21,200
And it's fueled by two of the coldest liquids, liquid oxygen and liquid hydrogen.

365
00:29:21,201 --> 00:29:28,200
Hydrogen has a really good quality that when it ignites, it releases most of its energy as heat.

366
00:29:28,201 --> 00:29:32,200
So when you combine liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen in a chemical reaction,

367
00:29:32,201 --> 00:29:37,200
that reaction explodes and all that energy gets turned into high temperature and high pressure,

368
00:29:37,201 --> 00:29:41,200
which is really what pushes the propellants through the nozzle.

369
00:29:41,201 --> 00:29:47,840
Two and a half weeks before the launch, workers top off the massive 900,000-gallon liquid

370
00:29:47,841 --> 00:29:51,880
oxygen storage tank on the northwest corner of the launch pad.

371
00:29:51,881 --> 00:29:56,740
Here, smoking can be particularly hazardous to your health.

372
00:29:56,741 --> 00:30:02,220
Behind me we have the liquid oxygen storage area, and you can see five tankers that are

373
00:30:02,221 --> 00:30:05,160
getting ready to push liquid oxygen into our storage sphere.

374
00:30:05,161 --> 00:30:08,600
Each tanker holds about 5,000 gallons of liquid oxygen.

375
00:30:08,601 --> 00:30:12,700
Today we took three waves of tankers, so that'll be a total of about 60,000 gallons today.

376
00:30:13,720 --> 00:30:18,940
The northeast corner of the launch pad houses an 850,000-gallon storage tank

377
00:30:18,941 --> 00:30:23,480
that keeps the liquid hydrogen cooled to minus 423 degrees Fahrenheit.

378
00:30:24,620 --> 00:30:29,780
By storing them as a liquid, the volume is much more condensed.

379
00:30:29,781 --> 00:30:33,020
If you can think of a beach ball-sized amount of gas,

380
00:30:33,420 --> 00:30:36,780
we can compress that down and liquefy it into about the size of a golf ball.

381
00:30:36,781 --> 00:30:40,020
And the same effect goes for storage on board the external tank.

382
00:30:41,340 --> 00:30:45,740
Ten hours before launch, NASA engineers begin fueling the external tank.

383
00:30:46,180 --> 00:30:51,320
We go through a phase called chill-down, which is basically moving cold gas through all the piping,

384
00:30:51,920 --> 00:30:55,600
and we'll be transferring about 1,400 gallons per minute up to the shuttle.

385
00:30:56,420 --> 00:30:58,380
Fueling is a three-hour-long process.

386
00:30:59,320 --> 00:31:04,360
Within the external tank, the liquids stay separated in two compartments right up until launch.

387
00:31:04,361 --> 00:31:11,280
The liquid oxygen tank holds 143,000 gallons, and the liquid hydrogen tank holds 383,000 gallons.

388
00:31:11,560 --> 00:31:15,200
This orange color that you see on the external tank is actually insulation.

389
00:31:15,380 --> 00:31:21,100
There's about an inch thick foam that's sprayed on when the tank is manufactured, and that keeps ice from forming.

390
00:31:22,200 --> 00:31:27,580
At T-minus 6.6 seconds, the liquids are finally funneled together in a combustion chamber.

391
00:31:28,540 --> 00:31:32,620
The hydrogen burns, creating water and heat with high thrust.

392
00:31:32,621 --> 00:31:38,260
They are mixed at a ratio of about six pounds of oxygen to one pound of hydrogen.

393
00:31:38,261 --> 00:31:42,220
They are then ignited and then their energy content is released.

394
00:31:42,221 --> 00:31:48,380
Shortly after liftoff, the engines are extracting the liquid oxygen and liquid hydrogen from

395
00:31:48,381 --> 00:31:52,800
the external tank at a rate of about 1,000 gallons per second.

396
00:31:52,801 --> 00:31:58,000
At that rate, you would empty a 10,000-gallon swimming pool in about 10 seconds.

397
00:31:58,001 --> 00:32:07,286
It is this energy that pushes the propellants out through the nozzle and it accelerates the steam, which is the byproduct of the combustion process, to about 9,000

398
00:32:07,287 --> 00:32:08,140
miles per hour.

399
00:32:09,340 --> 00:32:17,520
At a temperature as high as 6,000 degrees Fahrenheit, the steam generates 1.5 million pounds of thrust from the three main engines.

400
00:32:18,320 --> 00:32:23,760
And with some additional help from the solid rocket boosters, propels the shuttle into space.

401
00:32:24,660 --> 00:32:27,200
Standing by for solid rocket booster separation.

402
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:35,000
Temperatures in outer space are as low as 3 Kelvin, or minus 454 degrees Fahrenheit.

403
00:32:35,001 --> 00:32:44,000
NASA scientists must ensure anything sent into the deep freeze of space will not only survive, but thrive in the frigid environment.

404
00:32:44,001 --> 00:32:55,000
Since 1991, NASA's Hubble Space Telescope has peered into its bitterly cold surroundings, helping astronomers map out the universe.

405
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:07,000
To see even further, NASA is teaming up with industry to build the James Webb Space Telescope, or JWST.

406
00:33:07,001 --> 00:33:15,000
It will launch in 2013, and it really will be a significant advancement over the Hubble Space Telescope.

407
00:33:15,001 --> 00:33:20,000
Its mirror is seven times larger, which will give it tremendous collecting power.

408
00:33:20,001 --> 00:33:26,000
The JWST Infrared Space Telescope will orbit the sun one million miles from the Earth.

409
00:33:26,001 --> 00:33:27,017
from the Earth.

410
00:33:27,018 --> 00:33:31,900
There it will try to gather images of the first galaxies ever formed and

411
00:33:31,901 --> 00:33:35,200
search for other planets that may harbor life.

412
00:33:35,201 --> 00:33:38,500
But capturing this faint light creates a major

413
00:33:38,501 --> 00:33:40,860
engineering challenge.

414
00:33:40,861 --> 00:33:45,018
Your eye sees in the violet to the red wavelengths.

415
00:33:45,019 --> 00:33:48,360
However, the signatures we're looking for

416
00:33:48,361 --> 00:33:50,151
are well out into the infrared.

417
00:33:50,152 --> 00:33:53,560
To see those wavelengths, which are generally heat related,

418
00:33:53,561 --> 00:33:59,765
need a very cold telescope. The JWST must be cooled to cryogenic temperatures.

419
00:33:59,766 --> 00:34:00,560
Otherwise,

420
00:34:02,200 --> 00:34:07,118
heat from the telescope itself would essentially blind the sensors.

421
00:34:07,119 --> 00:34:08,880
So engineers at Northrop

422
00:34:08,881 --> 00:34:15,160
Grumman in Redondo Beach, California are using the deep freeze of space to their advantage.

423
00:34:15,161 --> 00:34:19,520
They're constructing a giant sun shield to block the heat of the sun and keep the telescope

424
00:34:19,521 --> 00:34:24,420
cooled down to the temperatures of its intensely cold environment.

425
00:34:24,421 --> 00:34:31,680
It's essentially a giant umbrella in space that has five different layers.

426
00:34:31,681 --> 00:34:37,340
The first layer, the sun facing layer, really takes the brunt of the sunlight.

427
00:34:37,341 --> 00:34:44,080
It cools the second layer and so on until we get down to almost absolute zero at the

428
00:34:44,081 --> 00:34:46,020
innermost or fifth layer.

429
00:34:46,021 --> 00:34:52,020
The shield will be 70 feet long by 45 feet wide, or about the size of a tennis court.

430
00:34:53,020 --> 00:34:56,120
This is our full-size development article for the SunShield.

431
00:34:56,340 --> 00:35:00,260
It's currently in a partially stowed configuration right now.

432
00:35:00,340 --> 00:35:01,760
It's folded up on the sides.

433
00:35:02,460 --> 00:35:05,580
The material for the SunShield is very thin plastic.

434
00:35:05,800 --> 00:35:07,940
It's cap timing made by DuPont.

435
00:35:08,240 --> 00:35:11,840
The thickness is about one-third that of the average human hair.

436
00:35:11,841 --> 00:35:17,840
Fully opened, the shield won't fit onto the rocket to be launched into orbit.

437
00:35:17,841 --> 00:35:24,840
So engineers at Northrop Grumman are testing the best methods for folding it up and deploying it in space.

438
00:35:24,841 --> 00:35:32,840
Once in orbit, the JWST will be one of NASA's largest and most complex achievements.

439
00:35:32,841 --> 00:35:40,840
The Boomerang Nebula, a cloud of gas and dust blown away from a star, is the coldest place in the universe.

440
00:35:40,841 --> 00:35:46,260
The cloud is 1 Kelvin, or minus 457.6 degrees Fahrenheit.

441
00:35:47,400 --> 00:35:50,080
Deep Freeze will return on Modern Marvelous.

442
00:35:52,080 --> 00:35:55,000
Nestled in the blistering heat of Scottsdale, Arizona,

443
00:35:55,980 --> 00:35:59,500
the Encore Life Extension Foundation is putting cold to the test

444
00:35:59,501 --> 00:36:01,380
in an ultimate science experiment.

445
00:36:02,800 --> 00:36:08,720
